  "slug": "graphene-engineering-5eb6",
  "summary": "Recent preprints show strain-induced pseudo-fields and gate-defined superlattices now serve as design parameters for valley and correlated states.",
  "synthesis": "Three of the four preprints control the low-energy electronic structure of graphene through mechanical strain or electrostatic gating. In wrinkled graphene nanoribbons, tight-binding and quantum transport calculations show gate-tunable valley filtering and splitting. In multilayer graphene with rhombohedral stacking, strain generates giant pseudo-electric fields that produce flat bands, enhanced correlations, and topological boundary states. In Bernal bilayer graphene, a patterned graphite gate defines a superlattice with miniband insulating states and electrostatically tunable band structure. The twisted bilayer Floquet study addresses valley polarization and Chern-number phase transitions through periodic driving rather than strain or gating. The new element is the use of strain-induced pseudo-fields and gate-defined superlattices as design parameters for valley and correlated states. The items do not establish a single mechanism across the systems, so the shared thread is control of band structure rather than a unified physical picture.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
